ommunity has been at the top of a deeply hierarchical system, even the ones who reject that system often still inherit certain mannerisms, secure cultural capital, or unconscious privilege that can read as arrogance to others, even if it’s not intended that way. At the same time, there really are Brahmins who are humble, self-critical, ashamed of caste history, or actively working against it—and many of those people are not visible in the same spaces because they may intentionally avoid traditional family functions or elite professional circles where caste performance happens. That doesn't invalidate your generational pattern; it just means the pattern might describe a very large, dominant slice rather than the entire pie. So why does this distinction matter? It matters because when we say "all Brahmins are like that," we unwittingly adopt the same logic that caste ideology uses—that identity determines character. It also can blind us to allies, or cause us to distrust someone before they've even said a word. That’s a practical cost, not just a philosophical one. But I don’t want to end on an abstract note. I hear the depth of what you're saying: the weight of generational experience is heavy, and it makes sense to be frustrated when someone seems to be explaining it away. I’m not explaining it away. I’m acknowledging that what you’re describing is a real, lasting social phenomenon rooted in historical power. The question I leave with you—and I'm not demanding an answer—is whether we can hold that reality while still leaving room for the possibility that the category "Brahmin" contains more variation than even a lifetime of consistent experience might reveal.
